.he "makekey (1)                  9/30/26                  makekey (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
makekey
- generate a key for crypt
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
makekey
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Makekey reads ten bytes from its standard input and writes thirteen
to its standard output.  The ten are a key of eight characters
followed by a salt of two; the thirteen are the string that
.bd
crypt (3)
returns for them.
.sp
It is the key generator for
.bd
crypt (1),
which forks it and talks to it down a pipe, and it is not meant to be
run by hand.  What it is for is that the transformation be slow - it
takes a noticeable part of a second - so that trying keys one at a
time against an enciphered file costs that much per key.  It is done
once per run, not once per byte, which is the point of the fork.
.sp
The salt is what makes two invocations with the same key give
different thirteen-character results.  Crypt passes the first two
characters of the key word as its salt, so it is the same two every
time for a given key; a password file picks its two elsewhere.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/usr/lib/makekey - where crypt (1) looks for it
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The answer is computed by
.bd
crypt (3),
the password hash - the older big-multiply one and not the DES one.
.sp
The hash walks a password to its NUL, and v7's makekey held the eight
key bytes in a buffer with no terminator, so a full eight character
key had the hash reading past the end of it until it found a zero
somewhere on the stack.  This one puts the NUL in after the eight
bytes, so exactly the eight key characters are hashed.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
crypt (1), crypt (3), login (1), passwd (1)
